+## 186.2 - (Fixed/Improved) Base AdvCiv active-World-Wonder exemption could make the AI retain remote captured cities, exhaust defenders, and return the preserved Wonder to a nearer rival
+
+Screenshots/files for this issue: [google drive folder link](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1tiU9J53BJqL92RBCBZ0oIv9uM4zyA6Tv?usp=sharing).
+
+This exemption came from Base AdvCiv, not AdvCiv-SAS. Its conquest condition, introduced under `advc.122` and later kept under `advc.ctr`, required `!kCity.hasActiveWorldWonder() && AI_isAwfulSite(kCity, true)` before the awful-site gate could force a raze. It was authored by f1rpo in Base AdvCiv history years before the AdvCiv-SAS changes. The KI#186 replacement for `AI_isAwfulSite` initially preserved that inherited active-World-Wonder exemption for the new long-term city evaluation. This could make the Wonder dominate the decision even when the captured city was too far away to defend durably. Briefly keeping the Wonder was not useful if a nearer rival could recapture the developed city after our army had been weakened there.
+
+BBAI testing in `BBAI_20260715T195321Z_load1.log` / `SASGameSummary_20260715T195321Z_load1.log` reproduced that failure with England's capture of population-18 Elephantine at distance 15. Its active Eiffel Tower prevented the long-term evaluation. England committed 15 defenders there by turn 230; Egypt killed at least 7 logged English defenders on the city plot and recaptured Elephantine on turn 233, only 6 turns after England had taken it. England gained no durable city or Wonder benefit and instead exhausted units to preserve the Eiffel Tower briefly for nearby Egypt.
+
+The conquest logic now has the default-enabled `SAS_AI_CITY_LONG_TERM_DISTANCE_OVERRIDE_ACTIVE_WORLD_WONDER_ENABLE`. An active World Wonder still protects the city's intrinsic site quality, but it no longer bypasses excessive same-area distance. This keeps a nearby Wonder city eligible for normal captured-city valuation while allowing a truly remote one to be razed, permanently denying the Wonder instead of donating it back after a costly defense. Barbarian, holy-city, and previously-owned-city exclusions remain unchanged.
+
+Save-file 453 provided the direct confirmation in `BBAI_20260716T082233Z_new1.log` / `SASGameSummary_20260716T082233Z_new1.log`. Inca captured population-17 Madrid with an active Statue of Zeus at same-area distance 13. The log reported `intrinsicSiteProtectedByActiveWorldWonder=1`, `distanceOverrideActiveWorldWonder=1`, `sameAreaDistanceLikelyToBenefit=0`, and `RAZE_DECISION ... action=RAZE`. The active Wonder therefore protected intrinsic quality exactly as intended but did not override the unsustainable distance result.
+
+The same save also tested whether the distance limit should be increased from 8 to 9. Population-17 Cordoba at distance 9 and `closeness=16` initially looked like an attractive bridgehead for conquering the nearby Spanish cities, so 9 seemed plausible. The limit-9 A/B retest in `BBAI_20260716T083454Z_load1.log` / `SASGameSummary_20260716T083454Z_load1.log` showed the opposite. Inca kept Cordoba with normal `finalValue=-89`; the Ottomans captured it immediately and killed 2 Incan Robotic Infantry. Inca recaptured it on turn 247, but the Ottomans captured it again on turn 248 and killed 5 Robotic Tanks, 2 more Robotic Infantry, and 1 Gunship. They then transferred the still-developed city back to Spain. Inca lost 10 advanced units defending and recapturing the apparent bridgehead without securing it, reproducing the exact remote-city trap that the distance gate is meant to prevent.
+
+The default distance limit therefore remains 8. It should not be raised merely because a distance-9 city looks valuable or could theoretically anchor further conquest: the slightly larger allowance was enough to split a powerful army around an exposed capture and give the developed city back to a nearer rival. The limit remains XML-tunable for other maps and rulesets, but the tested default favors durable military concentration over speculative bridgeheads.
 
 Fixed/improved with the help of GPT-5.6-Sol (on ChatGPT Codex) thanks.

